{"answer":"No. The argument is a contradiction proof: assuming a lowest-terms rational representation leads to both numerator and denominator being even, contradicting lowest terms. Thus the claim that sqrt(2) is rational is false; sqrt(2) is irrational. The inference that a and b are even is valid when the parity implication “if n² is even, then n is even” is supplied, as done below.","approaches":[{"description":"Direct contradiction argument from a supposed lowest-terms fraction, with the parity implication proved explicitly.","id":"parity-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It establishes that the assumed rational representation cannot exist."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-premise","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The proposed proof supposes a representation sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary reductio assumption that sqrt(2) is rational, instantiated by a lowest-terms integer fraction.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"id":"rationality-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["assume-fraction","square-equation","a-square-even","a-even","b-square-even","b-even","contradict-lowest-terms"],"statement":"Assume sqrt(2) is rational; then choose coprime integers a,b with b nonzero and sqrt(2)=a/b.","step_ids":["assume-fraction"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the equality in assume-fraction and multiplying by b².","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"square-equation-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"a²=2b².","step_ids":["square-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from square-equation-claim and the explicit odd-square calculation in a-even.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["square-equation-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"a is even.","step_ids":["a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from a-even-claim, square-equation-claim, and the explicit odd-square calculation in b-even.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["a-even-claim","square-equation-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"b is even.","step_ids":["b-square-even","b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction in contradict-lowest-terms discharges rationality-assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-premise","rationality-assumption","a-even-claim","b-even-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"irrationality-conclusion","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["conclude-irrational"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"assume-fraction","justification":"This is the temporary assumption for contradiction; a and b are coprime integers and b is nonzero.","statement":"Assume sqrt(2) is rational and write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["assume-fraction"],"id":"square-equation","justification":"Squaring gives 2=a²/b²; multiplication by b² is valid because b is nonzero.","statement":"a²=2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation"],"id":"a-square-even","justification":"The right-hand side is divisible by 2.","statement":"a² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-square-even"],"id":"a-even","justification":"If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, so a²=4k²+4k+1=2(2k²+2k)+1 would be odd, contradicting a-square-even.","statement":"a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation","a-even"],"id":"b-square-even","justification":"Write a=2c. Substitution into a²=2b² gives 4c²=2b², hence b²=2c².","statement":"b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["b-square-even"],"id":"b-even","justification":"The same odd-square calculation used in a-even shows that an integer with even square must be even.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["assume-fraction","a-even","b-even"],"id":"contradict-lowest-terms","justification":"If both a and b are even, then 2 is a common divisor, contrary to their being coprime.","statement":"This contradicts that a/b is in lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"id":"conclude-irrational","justification":"The temporary rationality assumption has produced a contradiction and is therefore false.","statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
